<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunni Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1464">1464</a>–<a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Ali was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> from 1464 to 1492 and the founder of the Songhai Empire. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in 1468 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> around 1473. Accounts of the Songhai Empire credit him with building the empire's military power, including its river fleet.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sunni Ali, also called Si Ali and Sonni Ali Ber (&quot;the Great&quot;), was the 15th ruler of the Sunni dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>. He was the son of Sonni Muhammad Da'o, who appears in the kinglists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-fattash">Tarikh al-Fattash</a> as the 10th Sonni ruler. His mother came from Fara, an area that was still heavily pagan, and Ali was raised in that setting. As a Sonni he also received an Islamic education, but he practised a syncretic, unorthodox faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Sunni Ali took the throne, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> already controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> basin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dendi">Dendi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a>. His first major conquest was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> since the Malian retreat a few decades earlier, whose Timbuktu-koi 'Umar asked for Songhai protection. Sunni Ali followed a repressive policy toward the scholars of Timbuktu, whom he associated with the Tuareg. He built a powerful fleet on the Niger and used it in 1473 to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>, which surrendered only after being reduced to starvation.</p><p>He tried to dig a canal of hundreds of kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> el Ma toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walata">Walata</a>, so that his fleet could be used against that town, but in 1483 he abandoned the project to defeat an invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a>. He also conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> lands called Nunu and the lands of Kunta, and he intended to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgu">Borgu</a> but could not. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a> and other nomadic peoples raiding within his borders. Gao was his main capital, but he was also based at Kukiya, Kabara and Tindirma, depending on where he campaigned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sunni Ali ruled urban Muslims and rural non-Muslims at a time when their traditional coexistence was being challenged. Because he combined African animism with a profession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, some writers describe him as outwardly or nominally Muslim, although he observed the prayers, fasted and gave alms. Toby Green notes that he did not permit the acceptance of Sharī’a in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>. The scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> wrote of him with fury, and al-Maghili said he worshipped idols and venerated trees and stones. Green argues that his relations with the clerics were less brutal than this picture, and funeral stelae from Kukiya cast doubt on the chroniclers, who wrote for the Askias who overthrew his dynasty.</p><p>He died on 6 November 1492, in circumstances that are a matter of conjecture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> says he drowned while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>; oral tradition holds that his sister's son, Askia Muhammad Ture, killed him. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-baru">Sunni Baru</a> was proclaimed king by the army commanders but was challenged by Askia, as he was not seen as a faithful Muslim, and Askia eventually defeated him and took power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sonni Ali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Songhai Empire was the largest empire in West African history and reached its height in the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under <b>Sunni Ali</b> and Askia Muhammad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
